{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CfrM9ESFhQ5dCxVCwCQnN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The problem is scaling, not the solver. Dataset 2's kernel and data are about 10⁵ times larger than dataset 1's, so the unweighted least-squares fit ignores dataset 1.\n\n**The numbers** (from running your setup):\n\n| | dataset 1 (rays) | dataset 2 (gravity) |\n|---|---|---|\n| max kernel entry | 0.010 | 270 |\n| RMS data | 0.065 | 961 |\n\nCGLS minimises ‖Am − y‖² with no per-block weighting. Dataset 2's residuals are on the order of 10³, so its squared misfit dominates the objective. Dataset 1's contribution is around 10⁻¹⁰ of the total, so the solver fits dataset 2 to 3e-9 and leaves dataset 1 unfit. Your `damp=1e-2` is also absolute, so it does nothing against kernel entries of order 10². That is why adding dataset 1 changes nothing: its rows are numerically invisible.\n\n**Why the model error stays at ~0.22:** dataset 1 alone is well-posed. It has 150 rays for 100 cells, full rank, condition number about 31, and the ray-only least-squares solution recovers `mtrue` to 7e-4. The gravity kernel is smooth and has a large null space (30 rows, 100 unknowns), and with dataset 1 effectively ignored, the solution sits in that null space. The 0.22 error is the gravity-only answer.\n\n**Fix:** weight each block by its noise standard deviation, so every row is in units of its own noise. I checked this in the same script:\n\n```python\ns1, s2 = 1e-5, 1e-1\nOw = pylops.VStack([pylops.MatrixMult(K1/s1), pylops.MatrixMult(K2/s2)])\nyw = np.concatenate([d1/s1, d2/s2])\nmw = pylops.optimization.basic.cgls(Ow, yw, niter=300, damp=1e-6)[0]\n```\n\nResults with weighting:\n- dataset 1 misfit: 1.1e-4 (was 6.3e-2)\n- dataset 2 misfit: 8.2e-5 (was 3.4e-9)\n- model error: 7.1e-4 (was 0.22)\n\nThe dataset 2 misfit is now worse, which is the correct trade-off. Before, dataset 2 was \"fitted exactly\" only because it dominated the objective. Both datasets now fit to roughly their noise levels.\n\nTwo notes:\n- Check the 1e8·1e2 unit factors in `K2`. They make gravity data about 10³, which is why the scale mismatch is so large. If the units are intended, weighting fixes the problem. If not, fix the kernel.\n- Noise weighting is the right default for joint inversion. Alternatively, normalise each block to unit norm, but that does not account for the different noise levels.\n\nI did not modify your script. The diagnostic code is in `$TMPDIR/diag.py`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":473,"cache_read_input_tokens":21974,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":473},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"20348de8-e8ef-4cbc-ba9a-7d302b825217","uuid":"05368acc-2a15-4cb8-9fdb-f17f97d62b9b","timestamp":"2026-10-09T08:28:49.223Z","request_id":"req_011CfrM9ECcjMfVSW1VUZ27J"}
