{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CfrMTaXz5BQLN7MbFToTC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The problem is `adj_int`, which isn't the adjoint of `fwd_int`. Changing `damp` or `niter` can't fix that, and a second problem (unweighted blocks) compounds it.\n\n**1. Wrong adjoint (the main bug).** `fwd_int` is a lower-triangular matrix `L` with entries `dz` on and below the diagonal. Its adjoint is `Lᵀ`, an upper-triangular matrix, which is a *reverse* cumsum. `adj_int` uses a forward cumsum, which is `L` itself, not `Lᵀ`. LSQR assumes `Aᵀ` is correct, so it solves the wrong normal equations and converges to the wrong model no matter how many iterations you run. A dot test shows it:\n\n```\ndot test bad: <Lx, w> = 1.7750   <x, Lᵀw> = 0.9449   # mismatch\ndot test ok : <Lx, w> = 1.7750   <x, Lᵀw> = 1.7750   # match\n```\n\nFix:\n```python\ndef adj_int(d):\n    return np.cumsum(d[::-1])[::-1] * dz\n```\n\n**2. Unweighted blocks.** LSQR minimizes absolute residuals, so the blocks must be scaled by their noise levels. Right now `Op2` has entries around 3e3 (its outputs are around 4e4) with noise 1.0, while `Op1` outputs are around 0.1 with noise 1e-4. The d2 residuals dominate the objective, which is why d2 fits essentially perfectly while d1 is ignored. Whitening each block by its noise standard deviation fixes that. Use scalar multiplication, since `Op / s` in PyLops means operator division:\n\n```python\nsig1, sig2 = 1e-4, 1.0\nOp = pylops.VStack([(1/sig1) * Op1, (1/sig2) * Op2])\ny = np.concatenate([d1 / sig1, d2 / sig2])\nminv = pylops.optimization.basic.lsqr(Op, y, niter=300, damp=1e-6)[0]\n```\n\nWith `damp=1e-3` on the unscaled system, damping is negligible against the roughly 1e4 scale of `Op2`, which is why tuning it had no effect. After the scaling, `damp` becomes a meaningful knob.\n\n**Results from my test run** (same synthetic setup, noise seed 7):\n\n| Variant | model error | d1 misfit | d2 misfit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Original | 0.269 | 8.9e-3 | 1.3e-5 |\n| Adjoint fixed only | 0.268 | 8.8e-3 | 2.2e-5 |\n| Adjoint fixed + noise-whitened | 0.039 | 4.2e-4 | 3.3e-5 |\n\nThe remaining 0.039 is expected. `Op1` is invertible, so `m` is essentially `diff(d1)/dz`, and the 1e-4 noise on `d1` is amplified by roughly `1/dz ≈ 120`. The residual error is therefore close to the noise floor of the data, not an algorithm problem. To go lower you would need regularization (for example, smoothing or a model-norm penalty) rather than more iterations."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":563,"cache_read_input_tokens":22016,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":563},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"101dec05-a000-4bce-86ed-47ffc80e6ffd","uuid":"15a79a8b-4bc8-4d6c-b8b4-89fad33d3688","timestamp":"2026-10-09T08:32:59.178Z","request_id":"req_011CfrMTaK5zRkHySFEPKDLe"}
